module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.identity_hashed_from_a_shared_mutable_path

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data identity_hashed_from_a_shared_mutable_path: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "identity_hashed_from_a_shared_mutable_path" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**an identity is computed from a SHARED MUTABLE PATH rather than from the bytes in hand, and the guard that is present guards the wrong object** (INVALID STATE: a writer serializes a value it holds in memory, writes it to a path that other writers also write, then asks a content-addressed store for the id of WHATEVER THAT PATH HOLDS WHEN THE STORE READS IT, and publishes that id as its own.",

    "HARM, two arms of one mechanism and the quiet one is the common one: a concurrent writer's O_TRUNC between the write and the read publishes the store's EMPTY object as the id, which is loud at the next decode; a completed foreign write publishes ANOTHER WRITER'S VALUE under this writer's admitted operation, which is silent -- the id is well formed, decodes, and names someone else's record. On a lease ledger the silent arm mints a grant from another writer's event.",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-09-11, gunbc.fabric_event_log event_log_append). The seat-ledger append wrote each event to <root>/event-<partition>.json under a root shared by every writer on the host, ran git hash-object -w on the path, and pushed the id with --force-with-lease. The harness then refused the healthy serving group at read: event e69de29bb2d1d6434b8b29ae775ad8c2e48c5391 undecodable -- git's empty blob as a partition head. A git-level replay of the same argv with concurrent writers on one scratch path produced both arms, the foreign-bytes arm far more often than the empty one; it was an uncommitted scratch reproduction, so its counts are not carried here, and the enrolled instrument that re-derives the mechanism is test.claim.fabric_event_log_append_real_execution. The empty case is what paged anyone.",

    "**DISTINGUISHING FACT, the one that makes it hard to see: THE GUARD WAS PRESENT AND CORRECT.** The compare-and-set on the head refused every stale append exactly as designed. It guards WHICH HEAD is replaced, and nothing guards WHAT the new object contains, so a reviewer who checks for the lease finds it and stops. Recognition rule: for any content-addressed publish, ask what binds the id to the value the writer holds. If the answer runs through a path, a fd or any other name another writer can reach between the write and the hash, the binding is absent and the guard is on the wrong object.",

    "DISTINCT FROM ITS NEIGHBOURS. ambient_process_state_read_by_a_concurrent_reader reads a PARAMETER from a shared cell; here the shared cell sits between a value and its IDENTITY, and the identity is then trusted as proof of the value. publish_over_a_path_a_reader_is_serving is the mirror image: a writer overwrites a path a reader has cached, so the digest certifies the file and not the served view; here the digest certifies the file and not the writer's value.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: silent wrongness for the foreign-bytes arm, which nothing contained; mitigatable for the empty arm, contained by the reader's typed decode refusal (which is correct and must not be relaxed to skip -- an empty event carries no parent, so skipping it drops the chain behind it).",

    "RUNG AFTER REPAIR, for the specimen's path only: structurally impossible. The append hands the serialized bytes to git on stdin (git.Plumbing HashObjectWriteStdin), so no name exists between the value and its id. Enrolled evidence: test.claim.fabric_event_log_append_real_execution on the local-repo wet lane, whose red control asserts that the path-routed form under the same clobber yields exactly the empty blob.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING FOR THE CLASS: MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE, AND NOT STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE -- decided, not left open, because an unstated ceiling reads as nothing more to do. The text-bearing form CAN be made path-free (HashObjectWriteStdin takes the bytes and has no path constructor), but that does not remove the invalid state, because the path-taking form must survive: build artifacts ARE files, and hashing a file is the legitimate operation for them. At the type, a legitimate caller (a file another process produced) and an illegitimate one (a file this caller just wrote from a value it holds) both hand over a FilePath, and nothing about a path records who wrote its bytes or whether another writer can reach it -- a file's bytes are shared mutable state by nature, and whether a given path is actually shared among concurrent writers is a deployment fact the source does not carry. The strongest available construction is to seal the path input of HashObjectWrite behind a sole_constructor artifact-path type minted only by named artifact producers (the admit_callers pattern extdeps.shell TransportScript uses), so that every new path-hashing caller is a conspicuous, counted edit to one list. That is still writable -- a caller can project the sealed path to a String and write text into it -- so it reaches mechanically preventable and stops there.",

    "SECOND INSTANCE, found by census when the row was filed and repaired alongside it (2026-09-11): gunbc.devboot.produce devboot_produce_build wrote its lease claim -- per-producer bytes, producer=<caller> -- to a store path keyed only by the token and hashed that path, so concurrent producers of one token could bind the lease ref to ANOTHER PRODUCER'S claim, the same lease-ledger harm as the specimen; its subject record and artifact digest, and gunbc.devboot.build stage_text_blob's six callers, took the same route. The claim site also ignored its own write result, so a failed write hashed whatever the file already held. All now hash from stdin with git.Plumbing HashObjectWriteStdin. EXECUTION COVERAGE OF THAT REPAIR IS PARTIAL AND THE TWO HALVES ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE. stage_text_blob is consumed by real execution in test.claim.devboot_text_blob_real_execution a_staged_entry_is_stored_as_exactly_its_bytes_in_the_worktree_repository_by_real_execution, which calls the production function and reads back the object it bound. devboot_produce_build's three bare-store sites -- the lease claim, the subject record and the artifact digest -- have NO execution control: the other test in that witness hashes producer=witness from stdin ITSELF at devboot_store_address and never enters the producer, so it exercises the ROUTE and not the caller, and reverting those three sites to the write-then-hash-the-path form leaves it green. Two facts make that gap real rather than an unwritten assertion: the subject record and the artifact digest are reached only after devboot_run_build_plan completes a real cargo release build, which the witness floor does not fund; and even a readback of the lease ref would not discriminate a revert, because a path-routed hash of the same bytes yields the same id unless a foreign writer lands between the producer's write and its hash, and that interleaving is not authorable from outside the producer. The mechanism's RED is therefore held once, by test.claim.fabric_event_log_append_real_execution's red control, and for these three sites the repair rests on call-site inspection.",

    "SECOND NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER FOR THE DEVBOOT SITES, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: a witness can enter gunbc.devboot.produce devboot_produce_build against a real bare store WITHOUT paying for a release build, and can place a foreign writer between that producer's serialize and its hash, so that the path-routed form goes red AT THE PRODUCER'S OWN SITE and the stdin form stays green. Adding assertions to the existing witness does not satisfy this trigger while the producer cannot be entered and the interleaving cannot be placed: that would re-measure the route, which is what the present coverage already does. The sealed-path trigger above subsumes this one for PREVENTION and does not replace it for EVIDENCE -- until one of the two lands, the devboot sites are covered by inspection only.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the path input of git.Plumbing HashObjectWrite accepts only a sealed artifact-path type whose admit_callers roster names the artifact producers, so that NO caller outside that roster can hash a path at all, and a caller that writes a value to a path it then hashes cannot be written without editing the roster. A census alone does not satisfy this trigger: it measures the population and refuses nothing. Population measured 2026-09-11 after both repairs: two production HashObjectWrite(path) sites, both hashing real build artifacts (gunbc.devboot.build hash_blob_file on the artifact path, and gunbc.devboot.produce's stored artifact) -- legitimate, because the bytes ARE the file -- and ZERO in-memory-text sites. The zero is a grep over call sites, not a wall; nothing stops the next author writing the pattern again, which is why the class stays at mitigatable until the sealed path lands.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.fabric_event_log", decl_name: "event_log_append", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.fabric_event_log_append_real_execution", decl_name: "an_append_publishes_its_own_bytes_when_the_scratch_path_is_clobbered_by_real_execution", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.fabric_event_log_append_real_execution", decl_name: "a_path_routed_hash_under_the_same_clobber_publishes_the_empty_blob_red_control_by_real_execution", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.devboot.build", decl_name: "stage_text_blob", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.devboot_text_blob_real_execution", decl_name: "a_staged_entry_is_stored_as_exactly_its_bytes_in_the_worktree_repository_by_real_execution", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.devboot.produce", decl_name: "devboot_produce_build", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
